These concepts form the complete foundation of camera calibration required for AI Engineering, Computer Vision, Robotics, SLAM, Autonomous Vehicles, AR/VR, and 3D Vision interviews and projects.

  Image formation in a camera is one of the most important concepts in computer vision, robotics, AR/VR, photogrammetry, and graphics. The process transforms a point in the 3D world into a 2D pixel on the camera image.

Let's build it step by step, mathematically and intuitively.

1. The Big Picture

A camera performs this transformation:

(Xw,Yw,Zw)→(Xc,Yc,Zc)→(x,y)→(u,v)(X_w,Y_w,Z_w) \rightarrow (X_c,Y_c,Z_c) \rightarrow (x,y) \rightarrow (u,v)

where:

Coordinate System	MeaningWorld Coordinates	Actual 3D position of object
Camera Coordinates	Position relative to camera
Image Plane Coordinates	Projection onto image plane
Pixel Coordinates	Actual image pixels

Thus,

3D→3D→2D→Pixel3D \rightarrow 3D \rightarrow 2D \rightarrow Pixel
2. Coordinate Systems
World Coordinate System

Assume a point in the world:

Pw=[XwYwZw]P_w = \begin{bmatrix} X_w\\ Y_w\\ Z_w \end{bmatrix}

Example:

Pw=[235]mP_w= \begin{bmatrix} 2\\ 3\\ 5 \end{bmatrix} m

This means the object is located at (2m,3m,5m).

Camera Coordinate System

The camera has its own coordinate frame.

Convention:

X-axis → right
Y-axis → down/up
Z-axis → forward along optical axis

If the camera moves or rotates, world coordinates must be converted into camera coordinates.

3. Extrinsic Transformation

This transformation tells:

"Where is the camera in the world?"

It consists of:

Rotation Matrix
R=[r11r12r13r21r22r23r31r32r33]R= \begin{bmatrix} r_{11}&r_{12}&r_{13}\\ r_{21}&r_{22}&r_{23}\\ r_{31}&r_{32}&r_{33} \end{bmatrix}
Translation Vector
t=[txtytz]t= \begin{bmatrix} t_x\\ t_y\\ t_z \end{bmatrix}

Then

Pc=RPw+tP_c = RP_w+t

Expanding:

[XcYcZc]=R[XwYwZw]+t\begin{bmatrix} X_c\\ Y_c\\ Z_c \end{bmatrix} = R \begin{bmatrix} X_w\\ Y_w\\ Z_w \end{bmatrix} + t

This gives coordinates in the camera frame.

Homogeneous Form

Very common in computer vision.

Pc=[Rt01]PwP_c= \begin{bmatrix} R&t\\ 0&1 \end{bmatrix} P_w

or

Pc=[R∣t]PwP_c = [R|t]P_w

The matrix

[R∣t][R|t]

is called the Extrinsic Matrix.

