5. Perspective Projection

Suppose

Pc=[XcYcZc]P_c = \begin{bmatrix} X_c\\ Y_c\\ Z_c \end{bmatrix}

Image plane is located at

z=fz=f

where

ff

is focal length.

Using similar triangles:

x Coordinate
xf=XcZc\frac{x}{f} = \frac{X_c}{Z_c}

Therefore

x=fXcZcx=f\frac{X_c}{Z_c}
y Coordinate
yf=YcZc\frac{y}{f} = \frac{Y_c}{Z_c}

Hence

y=fYcZcy=f\frac{Y_c}{Z_c}

So projection becomes

(x,y)=(fXcZc,fYcZc)(x,y)= \left( f\frac{X_c}{Z_c}, f\frac{Y_c}{Z_c} \right)

Notice:

Depth appears in denominator
ZcZ_c

This causes distant objects to appear smaller.

This is the essence of perspective.

6. Normalized Image Coordinates

Define:

xn=XcZcx_n=\frac{X_c}{Z_c} yn=YcZcy_n=\frac{Y_c}{Z_c}

Then

x=fxnx=f x_n y=fyny=f y_n

Normalized coordinates are independent of focal length.

7. Camera Intrinsic Matrix

The projection above gives coordinates in metric units (mm).

To convert to pixels, we use camera intrinsics.

Pixel Scaling

Suppose:

fxf_x = focal length in x pixels
fyf_y = focal length in y pixels

Image center:

(cx,cy)(c_x,c_y)

called principal point.

Then

u=fxxn+cxu=f_x x_n+c_x v=fyyn+cyv=f_y y_n+c_y
Matrix Form
K=[fx0cx0fycy001]K= \begin{bmatrix} f_x & 0 & c_x\\ 0 & f_y & c_y\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}

This is the Intrinsic Matrix.

8. Complete Projection Equation

Combining extrinsic and intrinsic transformations:

s[uv1]=K[R∣t][XwYwZw1]s \begin{bmatrix} u\\ v\\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = K [R|t] \begin{bmatrix} X_w\\ Y_w\\ Z_w\\ 1 \end{bmatrix}

This is the most famous equation in computer vision.

9. Projection Matrix

Define:

P=K[R∣t]P = K[R|t]

Then:

sp=PXs\mathbf{p}=P\mathbf{X}

where

p=[uv1]\mathbf{p}= \begin{bmatrix} u\\ v\\ 1 \end{bmatrix}

and

X=[XwYwZw1]\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} X_w\\ Y_w\\ Z_w\\ 1 \end{bmatrix}

PP is called the Camera Projection Matrix.

